","Saltatory conduction is a type of saltatory conduction in which the action potential ""jumps"" from one node of Ranvier to the next, bypassing the insulated internodal segments. This is common in large myelinated fibres, where the myelin sheath reduces capacitance and intracellular resistance, allowing saltatory conduction. In contrast, non-saltatory conduction occurs in unmyelinated fibres, where the action potential moves smoothly along the entire length of the fibre. This process is slower and less efficient than saltatory conduction.
