What is the effect of stimulation of arterial baroreceptors on the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of vagal motor neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). This occurs because baroreceptors detect changes in blood pressure and send signals to the NTS via the vagus nerve. When blood pressure increases, baroreceptor activity increases, leading to an increase in vagal neuron activity. The vagal motor neurons then transmit inhibitory signals to the cardiovascular system, causing vasodilation and decreased heart rate, thereby reducing blood pressure. Thus, the effect of arterial baroreceptor stimulation on the activity of neurons in the NTS is an increase in vagal neuron activity, which ultimately leads to a decrease in blood pressure.  

$\boxed{\text{Stimulation of baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increases vagal neuron activity in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS).}}$
