"Calculate the power output of a Galvanic cell consisting of a standard hydrogen electrode and a standard copper electrode at 298 K when the concentration of copper ions in the cathode half-cell is 0.1 M and hydrogen gas pressure in the anode half-cell is at 1 bar. The standard reduction potentials for the hydrogen and copper half-reactions are +0.0 V and +0.34 V, respectively.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Calculate the power output of a Galvanic cell consisting of a standard hydrogen electrode and a standard copper electrode at 298 K when the concentration of copper ions in the cathode half-cell is 0.1 M and hydrogen gas pressure in the anode half-cell is at 1 bar. The standard reduction potentials for the hydrogen and copper half-reactions are +0.0 V and +0.34 V, respectively.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the power output of the galvanic cell, we follow these steps:

1. **Write the half-reactions and their standard potentials:**
   - **Anode half-reaction (oxidation):** \( \text{H}_2(g) \rightarrow 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2e^- \) with \( E_0 = 0.0 \, \text{V} \)
   - **Cathode half-reaction (reduction):** \( \text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}(s) \) with \( E_0 = 0.34 \, \text{V} \)

2. **Calculate the cell potential ( \( E_{\text{cell}} \)) using the Nernst equation:**
   \[
   E_{\text{cell}} = E_0 - \frac{2.3 RT}{nF} \log \frac{[\text{H}^+]^2 P_{\text{H}_2}}{\text{Cu}^{2+}}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   - \( R = 8.3 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{kJ/mol·K} \)
   - \( T = 298 \, \text{K} \)
   - \( n = 2 \)
   - \( F = 96.5 \, \text{kJ/V·mol} \)
   - \( P_{\text{H}_2} = 1 \, \text{bar} \)
   - \( \text{Cu}^{2+} = 0.1 \, \text{M} \)
   - \( \text{H}^+ = 10^{-7} \, \text{M} \) (assuming neutral pH)

   \[
   E_{\text{cell}} = 0.34 \, \text{V} - \frac{2.3 \times 8.3 \times 10^{-3} \times 298}{2 \times 96.5} \log \frac{(10^{-7})^2 \times 1}{0.1}
   \]
   \[
   E_{\text{cell}} = 0.34 \, \text{V} - 0.029 \, \text{V} \log (10^{-13})
   \]
   \[
   E_{\text{cell}} = 0.34 \, \text{V} + 0.29 \, \text{V} = 0.63 \, \text{V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the power output ( \( P \)) using the formula:**
   \[
   P = -nF \times E_{\text{cell}}
   \]
   Substituting the values:
   \[
   P = -2 \times 96.5 \, \text{kJ/V·mol} \times 0.63 \, \text{V}
   \]
   \[
   P = -126.6 \, \text{kJ/mol}
   \]
   The negative sign indicates that the cell is doing work (exergonic).

**Final Answer:**
The power output of the galvanic cell is \(\boxed{-126.6 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).
