A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the maximum height reached by the particle, we use the kinematic equation for a uniformly decelerating object:  
\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2as \]  
where:  
- \( v = 0 \) (final velocity at maximum height),  
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity),  
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity, negative as it opposes the upward motion),  
- \( s \) is the distance (height) from the ground.  

Substituting the known values:  
\[ 0^2 = (20 \, \text{m/s})^2 + 2(-9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2) \cdot s \]  
\[ 0 = 400 \, \text{m}^2/\text{s}^2 - 19.6 \, \text{m/s}^2 \cdot s \]  
\[ 19.6 \, \text{m/s}^2 \cdot s = 400 \, \text{m}^2/\text{s}^2 \]  
\[ s = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.40 \, \text{m} \]  

Thus, the maximum height reached is \(\boxed{20.40 \, \text{m}}\).
