","The coordination geometry of the iron (III) ion in iron (III) tetraphenylporphyrin complex is **square planar**. This is because the iron (III) ion forms four coordinate bonds with the four nitrogen atoms of the porphyrin ring, and the axial ligand occupies the fifth coordination site. The axial ligand can vary depending on the specific complex, but in the case of iron (III) tetraphenylporphyrin, it is typically a chloride ion (Cl⁻). The five coordinate geometry is best described as square pyramidal, but the axial ligand occupies an equatorial position in the square, making the coordination geometry square planar. 

The coordination geometry is influenced by:
1. The ligand field strength: The strength of the ligand field (porphyrin in this case) influences the splitting of the d orbitals and thus the geometry adopted by the metal ion.
2. The size of the metal ion: Larger metal ions prefer more equatorial coordination due to greater steric repulsion between axial ligands.
3. The presence of axial ligands: The presence of an axial ligand influences the geometry, as it occupies one of the five coordination sites.

Thus, the coordination geometry is a balance between electronic effects and steric effects. 

\boxed{\text{The coordination geometry is square planar due to the four nitrogen atoms occupying equatorial positions and the axial ligand occupying the fifth coordination site.}}
