\[
y_i = \frac{p_i (1 - f)}{p_i (1 - f) + p_j (1 - h)},
\]
where:
- \( y_i \) is the mass fraction of the first fuel (\( C_aH_b \)) in the mixture that is required for the mixture to be flammable.
- \( p_i \) is the flammability limit of the first fuel (\( C_aH_b \)) in the mixture.
- \( f \) is the flammability limit of the second fuel (\( C_dH_e \)) in the mixture.
- \( p_j \) is the mass fraction of the second fuel (\( C_dH_e \)) in the mixture.

This model helps predict the flammability limits of mixtures by accounting for the relative amounts of flammable materials present.

