","The selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) is a crucial parameter in determining the amplification of enantiomeric excess (e.e) in the dimerization of partially resolved substrates. A selectivity factor \( s > 1 \) indicates that the homochiral dimer formation is faster than the meso dimer formation, leading to preferential formation of the desired enantiomer. The selectivity factor directly affects the ee amplification by influencing the ratio of the two enantiomers in the final product. For example, a selectivity factor of \( s = 10 \) means the homochiral dimer forms 10 times faster than the meso dimer. This selectivity enhances the selectivity of the reaction, leading to higher ee in the final product. 

The selectivity factor \( s \) is directly related to the ee amplification by the following relationship:
\[
\text{Selectivity factor} = s = \frac{k_e}{k_m} = \frac{1 + \text{Selectivity factor}}{1 + \frac{1}{s}}
\]
Thus, a selectivity factor \( s > 1 \) results in an increase in the ee of the product. 

\[
\boxed{\text{The selectivity factor } s \text{ determines the extent to which the diastereoselectivity of the dimerization is amplified, leading to higher enantiomeric excess (e.e.) in the final product.}}
\]
