","The electrokinetic potential (\( \xi \)) of colloidal particles is highly dependent on the pH of the solution. At a given ionic strength, the isoelectric point (IEP) determines the pH at which the colloidal particles are electrically neutral, and thus, \( \xi = 0 \). Below the IEP, the particles are positively charged, while above the IEP, they are negatively charged. This is because the pH influences the degree of dissociation of functional groups on the particle surface, which in turn affects the overall charge. Thus, the electrokinetic potential varies with pH as follows:  
- At pH < IEP, \( \xi \) becomes positive and increases sharply.  
- At pH = IEP, \( \xi \) reaches zero.  
- At pH > IEP, \( \xi \) becomes negative and increases sharply.  

The magnitude of \( \xi \) also depends on the specific ions present in the solution, as they can either shield or unscreen the particle charge, affecting the electroosmotic mobility.  

\boxed{\text{The electrokinetic potential varies with pH, reaching zero at the IEP and increasing symmetrically on either side.}}
