","Isotopologues are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms, but share the same connectivity. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}\text{H}_4\) and \(^{13}\text{C}\text{H}_4\) are isotopologues because they both have a single carbon atom and four hydrogen atoms, but differ in the isotope of carbon.  

In contrast, isotopomers are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms and also differ in their connectivity. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}\text{H}_4\) and \(^{13}\text{C}^{13}\text{C}\text{H}_2\text{H}_2\) are not isotopologues because they differ in connectivity (e.g., \(^{13}\text{C}^{13}\text{C}\text{H}_2\text{H}_2\) has two carbon atoms, while \(^{13}\text{C}\text{H}_4\) has one).  

Thus, the key difference is that isotopologues differ only in isotopic composition, whereas isotopomers differ in both isotopic composition and connectivity.
