","In molecular orbital theory, the key difference between σ molecular orbitals and π molecular orbitals lies in their electron density distribution:  
- A σ molecular orbital has electron density concentrated directly between the nuclei, aligned directly along the internuclear axis.  
- A π molecular orbital has electron density concentrated *above and below* the plane of the bonded atoms, perpendicular to the internuclear axis.  

This difference arises from the symmetry and orientation of the atomic orbitals that combine to form the molecular orbitals. For example:  
- σ orbitals are formed by the head-on (σ) overlap of atomic orbitals, whereas π orbitals result from the side-by-side (π) overlap of atomic orbitals.  

$\boxed{\text{A σ orbital has electron density directly between the nuclei, while a π orbital has electron density above and below the plane of the bonded atoms.}}$
